        "detail": "Here is the full status picture:\n\n---\n\n**1. launchd state**\n\n- Service `com.trusty.console` is **running**, PID **86695**, state active.\n- `launchctl list` confirms: exit code `0` (clean), managed by launchd.\n\n**2. HTTP liveness**\n\n- `GET /api/console/services` → **HTTP 200**, responding correctly.\n- Response JSON shows three downstream services all healthy:\n\n| Service | Version | Status | URL |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| trusty-search | 0.24.4 | running | 127.0.0.1:7878 |\n| trusty-memory | 0.15.2 | running | 127.0.0.1:7070 |\n| trusty-analyze | 0.7.0 | running | 127.0.0.1:7879 |\n\n**3. Serving PID / stray process situation**\n\nThe stray unmanaged process from earlier is **still present**:\n\n| PID | Bind address | Managed by launchd? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **79806** | `0.0.0.0:7788` (all interfaces) | No — unmanaged |\n| **86695** | `127.0.0.1:7788` (loopback only) | Yes — com.trusty.console |\n\nBoth are still alive and both are listening on `:7788`. The `curl` to `127.0.0.1:7788` is being answered — given that PID 86695 binds specifically to `127.0.0.1` and PID 79806 binds to `0.0.0.0` (wildcard), on macOS the loopback-specific bind on 86695 takes precedence for connections to `127.0.0.1`, so the launchd-managed process (86695) is almost certainly the one actually serving the request. The stray 79806 would intercept connections arriving on any other interface.\n\nThe stray PID 79806 has not been killed — as requested, no action was taken.",
