        "detail": "Understood — and that's a cleaner principle than where my plan landed. Let me reflect it back so we're locked:\n\n> **HTTP is implemented exactly once — in trusty-console.** The console is the single HTTP surface (web UI + API). The services **stay as JSON-RPC-over-stdio (MCP)** — no per-service HTTP, no service-rendered HTML. The console acts as a **JSON-RPC/stdio client** to each service, requests metrics via their existing tools, and renders the native HTML tabs itself.\n\nSo my earlier \"console-HTTP calls service-HTTP\" was the redundancy you're rejecting — dropped. The console talks to each service over stdio JSON-RPC, not HTTP.\n\nThis is a structural change (the console becomes an MCP-stdio client/supervisor rather than an HTTP aggregator), so I'm scoping it properly before building — **not** to ask you more multiple-choice questions, but to nail the engineering: which stdio JSON-RPC tools each service exposes for metrics, the persistent-connection/process model (the console holds a long-lived stdio pipe per service rather than re-spawning per request), and how this coexists with / supersedes the services' current HTTP daemons. I'll come back with a tight plan and start building.\n\nOne scope flag I'll proceed on unless you say otherwise: I'll make the **console** the single HTTP/UI surface and have it talk to services purely over stdio JSON-RPC — but I'll **leave the services' existing HTTP daemons in place for now** (other things may call them) and treat fully retiring them as a follow-up, rather than ripping them out in this pass.",
